WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
Raw materials inside a typical passenger tire cost perhaps $15 while the finished tire sells

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:11.700
for well over 100. This is First Principles Daily, episode 130. That gap is not sitting

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.620
in the physics of rubber or steel. It shows up in how a tire is compounded, assembled,

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:23.605
moved, and sold. The chain starts with plantations and petrochemical plants. Makers buy natural

00:00:23.605 --> 00:00:28.565
rubber from the first and synthetic rubber from the second. Then mix each batch with carbon

00:00:28.565 --> 00:00:32.205
black or silica, oils, and curatives such as sulfur.

00:00:32.825 --> 00:00:38.345
Those compounds are extruded or calendared into treads, sidewalls, and inner liners, plus

00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:42.165
the rubber coated steel belts and textile plies that become the carcass.

00:00:42.865 --> 00:00:48.005
The inner liner is a specialized halibutal rubber sheet, chosen because air leaks through

00:00:48.005 --> 00:00:48.545
it slowly.

00:00:49.125 --> 00:00:54.405
The bead cores are high tensile steel wire coated with brass alloys, so the wire bonds

00:00:54.405 --> 00:00:55.545
during vulcanization.

00:00:55.545 --> 00:01:01.145
Each of those choices is a real material and a real process step, repeated at scale.

00:01:01.605 --> 00:01:04.045
None of them is where most of the retail price lives.

00:01:04.645 --> 00:01:09.725
The pieces go on to a drum and become a green tire, then into a curing press at roughly

00:01:09.725 --> 00:01:13.265
150 degrees Celsius for about 20 minutes under pressure.

00:01:13.845 --> 00:01:19.385
That press work is capital heavy, and it looks much the same across hundreds of factories worldwide.

00:01:19.385 --> 00:01:25.545
After the press, the tire enters brand-owned distribution, independent wholesalers and retail

00:01:25.545 --> 00:01:27.505
shops that also mount and balance.

00:01:28.085 --> 00:01:32.925
The shop's price carries inventory holding, warranty service, and the labor of fitting

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:33.825
rubber to a wheel.

00:01:34.505 --> 00:01:38.785
Branding and marketing split one tire from another even when the construction underneath

00:01:38.785 --> 00:01:39.465
is similar.

00:01:40.065 --> 00:01:43.765
What reaches the driver is the whole stack, not the sum of the inputs.

00:01:43.765 --> 00:01:49.305
boats. After the curing press, cost multiplies through handling, storage, and service bundled

00:01:49.305 --> 00:01:54.545
onto the tire. A driver buying four tires is also paying for regional warehouses that

00:01:54.545 --> 00:01:59.645
hold dozens of sizes, for the retailer's floor space, and for the shop's equipment and

00:01:59.645 --> 00:02:05.005
labor to balance each wheel. Those steps grew up around a standing assumption. A tire

00:02:05.005 --> 00:02:09.645
is a complex finished good that needs expert installation and a brand specific warranty.

00:02:09.645 --> 00:02:15.065
So the price treats every tire as already carrying that full service overhead, even

00:02:15.065 --> 00:02:16.345
before it leaves the factory.

00:02:16.985 --> 00:02:19.485
The magic wand number asks a simpler question.

00:02:20.085 --> 00:02:24.365
If you could arrange the raw atoms into the finished tire for free, what would you still have

00:02:24.365 --> 00:02:24.745
to pay?

00:02:25.425 --> 00:02:26.325
Only the commodities.

00:02:27.485 --> 00:02:30.365
Published composition figures give a rough split by weight.

00:02:30.865 --> 00:02:33.145
Rubber is about 41% of the tire.

00:02:33.625 --> 00:02:36.805
Carbon black and silica fillers are about 30%.

00:02:36.805 --> 00:02:41.005
Reinforcing steel and textile cords are about 15%.

00:02:41.005 --> 00:02:44.265
Oils, curatives, and other chemicals make up the rest.

00:02:44.845 --> 00:02:49.625
Commodity prices move, so this is a back of the envelope sum, not a measured invoice.

00:02:50.305 --> 00:02:56.405
Using current market ranges for natural rubber, styrene butadiene rubber, carbon black, and steel cord,

00:02:56.945 --> 00:02:59.765
the materials land in the low tens of dollars per tire.

00:03:00.325 --> 00:03:04.505
The energy for mixing and vulcanization does not move that estimate very far.

00:03:04.505 --> 00:03:10.885
car. Walk the pieces. If natural rubber trades near $2 per kilogram, an attire holds roughly

00:03:10.885 --> 00:03:16.925
4 kilograms of rubber compounds. The rubber alone sits near $8. Carbon black and

00:03:16.925 --> 00:03:22.945
silica at commodity rates add another $4 to $6. Steel cord and textile plies contribute

00:03:22.945 --> 00:03:29.225
perhaps $3 more. The remaining chemicals plus modest energy for the press bring the total

00:03:29.225 --> 00:03:36.965
near $15. That $15 is the material floor, the Magic 1 number for one passenger tire. Take a

00:03:36.965 --> 00:03:44.505
finished tire that reaches the customer at $150. Divide 150 by 15 and you get 10. A rough

00:03:44.505 --> 00:03:50.905
idiot index of about 10. The tire costs roughly 10 times the materials inside it. That is an estimate

00:03:50.905 --> 00:03:56.845
you can follow, not a factory audit. The gap is not inside the vulcanization press or the steel belt

00:03:56.845 --> 00:04:02.605
calendar. It opens in the steps after cure, moving finished tires through regional warehouses,

00:04:03.165 --> 00:04:08.585
a retailers margin for stocking many brands and many sizes, mounting and balancing folded

00:04:08.585 --> 00:04:13.385
into the same price. A brand premium that lets one maker charge more than another

00:04:13.385 --> 00:04:19.105
when the material lists are nearly the same. Each stage adds cost because the tire is treated

00:04:19.105 --> 00:04:23.965
as a finished good whose value is service and perception, not the atoms in the rubber.

00:04:23.965 --> 00:04:31.185
From that $15 floor to a $150 counter price, roughly $135 is left over.

00:04:31.725 --> 00:04:36.305
The largest single slice of that remainder is the retailer's margin, plus the bundled

00:04:36.305 --> 00:04:37.225
mounting service.

00:04:37.805 --> 00:04:42.505
Both scale with the assumption that every tire must be selected, stocked, and installed

00:04:42.505 --> 00:04:44.405
through the same multi-brand channel.

00:04:45.025 --> 00:04:50.145
Inventory risk across dozens of sizes and speed ratings compounds the carrying cost before

00:04:50.145 --> 00:04:51.605
the tire ever meets the wheel.

00:04:51.605 --> 00:04:56.284
A redesigned aimed at that floor would not start by inventing a new polymer.

00:04:56.824 --> 00:04:59.104
It would pull the tire apart from the service bundle.

00:04:59.684 --> 00:05:04.124
If mounting and balancing were priced and performed on their own, the tire could be sold

00:05:04.124 --> 00:05:05.724
closer to what it costs to make.

00:05:06.344 --> 00:05:11.184
A maker trying that first move would still have to pass the same regulatory test for treadwear,

00:05:11.664 --> 00:05:13.344
traction, and temperature resistance.

00:05:13.904 --> 00:05:18.524
The change is that certification cost would sit on the tire alone, instead of being spread

00:05:18.524 --> 00:05:20.104
across a bundled service price.

00:05:20.104 --> 00:05:25.504
Second, more shared sizes and constructions across brands would shrink what each retailer

00:05:25.504 --> 00:05:26.644
must keep on hand.

00:05:27.184 --> 00:05:31.564
Carrying costs now compounds all the way down the chain because the catalog is wide.

00:05:32.164 --> 00:05:37.184
That second move needs agreement on a narrower set of approved constructions, so one regional

00:05:37.184 --> 00:05:40.444
plant can run longer campaigns without constant changeovers.

00:05:41.044 --> 00:05:46.184
Third, direct distribution or regional micro factories that cure to local demand instead

00:05:46.184 --> 00:05:49.784
instead of to a forecast, would shorten the distance from press to vehicle.

00:05:50.304 --> 00:05:55.364
That third move needs enough local volume to keep a curing press busy, and idle presses

00:05:55.364 --> 00:05:57.284
the largest fixed cost in the sequence.

00:05:57.904 --> 00:06:02.464
None of these moves erases safety certification, or the capital tied up in curing presses.

00:06:03.064 --> 00:06:06.504
The tire must still meet load, speed, and durability standards.

00:06:06.984 --> 00:06:11.184
The raw material floor cannot fall below the commodity prices of rubber and steel.

00:06:11.184 --> 00:06:16.084
What can change is the assumption that every tire must travel the same long path through

00:06:16.084 --> 00:06:18.424
branding and retail before it touches the road.

00:06:19.064 --> 00:06:21.664
Each step has real engineering and commercial friction.

00:06:22.344 --> 00:06:26.124
None of them requires new materials or a break with the physics of vulcanization.

00:06:26.784 --> 00:06:30.144
They attack only the layers between the vulcanized tire and the driver.

00:06:31.205 --> 00:06:35.845
Service and branding costs stay invisible until someone calculates the material floor first.

00:06:36.445 --> 00:06:40.065
Once that floor is on the table, the rest of the sticker has to explain itself.

00:06:40.065 --> 00:06:46.125
where houses, floor space, fitting labor, and the premium for a name are no longer hiding inside

00:06:46.125 --> 00:06:51.305
what a tire costs. They are a separate stack and a separate stack can be shortened,

00:06:51.625 --> 00:06:57.505
unbundled or refused. The second principle is wider than rubber. Any product whose price is

00:06:57.505 --> 00:07:02.165
set by distribution layers rather than by its own atoms invites someone to cut the path

00:07:02.165 --> 00:07:07.445
between the last real process step and the customer. For a tire, that last real step

00:07:07.445 --> 00:07:13.365
is the cure. The invitation is the stretch from press to car, not a mystery inside the compound.

00:07:14.405 --> 00:07:19.845
The hard parts do not disappear just because the idiot index is high. Certification still has

00:07:19.845 --> 00:07:24.585
to be earned tire by tire. Presses still have to run full enough to pay for themselves.

00:07:25.325 --> 00:07:30.785
A narrower size range only helps if enough drivers will accept that range. A separated mounting

00:07:30.785 --> 00:07:35.905
charge only helps if the driver can still get the wheel on the car safely. Those constraints are

00:07:35.905 --> 00:07:40.605
why the post-cure stack has lasted. They are not approved that the stack is the physics.

00:07:41.631 --> 00:07:46.071
Tomorrow, this show comes back with another concrete case or another open opportunity.

00:07:46.731 --> 00:07:50.851
On tires, the first signal that someone is testing this path would be a credible plan

00:07:50.851 --> 00:07:56.111
to sell a narrow range of sizes, directly from regional plants, with mounting handled

00:07:56.111 --> 00:08:01.711
as a separate line item. That's first principles daily for today. If this changed how you see

00:08:01.711 --> 00:08:05.971
what things cost, share it with someone who depreciated, and subscribe wherever you

00:08:05.971 --> 00:08:06.251
listen.

